{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>He shall not alter it nor exchange it, good for bad or bad for good.</b> The reason for this is that the Torah descended to the end of a person's mind, which is immersed in the evil impulse (yetzer ha-ra); what he vowed and consecrated — perhaps he will change his mind and reconsider and wish to exchange the animal he consecrated for one inferior to it. For since it is consecrated with the sanctity of the body and he cannot redeem it, he might exchange it; therefore Scripture categorically stated that he may not exchange at all — good for bad or bad for good — for if He permitted him to exchange bad for good he would come to exchange good for bad and say, 'it is good.' And if he does exchange, Scripture penalized him and said: <b>then it and its exchange shall be holy.</b> And for this very same reason Scripture said concerning one who consecrated his house and wishes to redeem it, that he must add a fifth — for even though he vowed and consecrated, it is possible he will reconsider; therefore Scripture imposed upon him that he must add a fifth if he redeems it, as it is written: ואם המקדיש יגאל את ביתו ויסף חמישית כסף ערכך — 'And if he who consecrated it redeems his house, he shall add a fifth of the money of your valuation.' And so wrote the Ramban, of blessed memory."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:29:02.586632794Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}